# The origin this deployment publishes: canonical, social, and later sitemap URLs are built from it, and a release
# build refuses to run without a valid value (`scripts/check-site-url.ts`). It is public configuration, never a secret,
# and it is never derived from a request's Host header. A prerendered page bakes the build-time value into its HTML, so
# the deployed Worker must carry the same value (`wrangler.jsonc` `env.preview.vars`); changing only the Worker variable
# cannot rewrite what was already generated. Production uses https://caniupdate.dev, the remote preview uses its own
# workers.dev hostname. Plain http is accepted only for an address that is this machine's own — localhost or a
# LAN/Tailscale IP literal — so a page opened from another device renders matching canonical links:
# NUXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://100.122.224.20:5173

# The Nuxt Worker owns no AI setting, no provider credential, and no D1 binding. A briefing submission crosses the
# private `BRIEFING_SERVICE` binding to the briefing Worker, which reads its own local settings from the git-ignored
# `workers/briefing/.dev.vars` beside its Wrangler config (or from a Worker secret when deployed). Nuxt still reads
# this `.env`, but none of the names below belong to it any more, and nothing is copied into `.output/server/.dev.vars`.

# Local fixture: point the three briefing-Worker settings at a loopback OpenAI-compatible endpoint and treat its output
# as a test double rather than inference. The values live in `workers/briefing/.dev.vars`, not here:
#   AI_BRIEFING_ENDPOINT="http://127.0.0.1:8787/v1/chat/completions"
#   AI_BRIEFING_API_KEY="local-fixture-key"
#   AI_BRIEFING_MODEL="local-fixture-model"

# Real inference uses the same three names in the briefing Worker's environment. The endpoint is the complete
# OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions URL: its path must end with /chat/completions, and query parameters are rejected.
#   AI_BRIEFING_ENDPOINT=https://opencode.ai/zen/go/v1/chat/completions
#   AI_BRIEFING_API_KEY=<key>
#   AI_BRIEFING_MODEL=deepseek-v4-flash
# OpenRouter alternative: https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/chat/completions with deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash.

# Evidence provider credentials belong to the private evidence Worker: an optional GITHUB_TOKEN goes in
# `workers/evidence/.dev.vars` for local runs, or a secret on the deployed evidence Worker. Do not copy AI credentials
# there, and do not put a real provider key in this file.

# Optional logs + traces export directly to Grafana Cloud over OTLP/HTTP JSON; no container or Collector.
# Nuxt configuration belongs to the frontend server's private runtime config:
#   NUXT_OTEL_ENDPOINT=https://otlp-gateway-<region>.grafana.net/otlp
#   NUXT_PUBLIC_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=true
#   NUXT_OTEL_HEADERS={"authorization":"Basic <base64-instanceId:token>"}
# Configure deployed authentication as Worker secrets; never put real credentials in this example file.
# The application-side privacy policy sanitizes records before export.
# Only telemetryEnabled is public. Browser logs/traces go through same-origin /api/telemetry/v1/{logs,traces};
# never place the OTLP endpoint or authentication headers in NUXT_PUBLIC_* configuration.
# The briefing Worker owns its separate local setting in workers/briefing/.dev.vars:
#   OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:4318
#   OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS={"authorization":"Basic <instanceId:token>"}   # hosted-gateway auth; a Worker secret
# Unconfigured runtimes retain console diagnostics without remote export. The evidence Worker has no new exporter.
